  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1240 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nNumerous showers are expected across on Friday as the strong upper \nshortwave trough moves northeast over our area. Moderate to briefly \nheavy rain is expected with the showers. Most of the showers will be \nmoving northeast out of the area by late afternoon and evening. With \nthe rain and widespread cloud cover, not much of a temperature \nrecover is expected during the day. Have trimmed NBM highs to the \nlower to mid 60s and this is more in line with the GFS and NAM MOS. \nA few showers will be possible on Saturday as a minor impulse aloft \nmoves northeast into the southern Plains and North Texas. \nTemperatures will be warmer Saturday but considerable cloud cover is \nexpected to persist given the ample low-level moisture.\n\nThe warming trend will continue Sunday as a shortwave upper ridge \nmoves east over Texas, out ahead of a large scale upper low and \ntrough moving over the western CONUS. Should have skies becoming \npartly cloudy Sunday afternoon with a south breeze, with highs \nmostly in the upper 70s to lower 80s.  \n\nAttention then turns to the strong system for early next week. An \nupper low in the southern portion of the aforementioned western \nCONUS trough is progged to move southeast across the Four Corners \narea on Monday and into northern New Mexico. Medium range models \nhave come into better agreement with with the system through Monday, \nbut then have notable differences with its subsequent track Tuesday \ninto Wednesday. Latest indications are for this system to track far \nenough south to enable a band of convection to develop along \nassociated cold front as it moves across our area Monday evening \ninto Monday night. Temperatures look seasonably warm Monday ahead of \nthe front with highs in the upper 70s and lower 80s. \n\nA more progressive movement with the upper low and trough would \nresult in a scenario for our area with gusty northwest winds and \ncooler temperatures on Tuesday. Our area would then have the \npotential for a couple of nights with lows in the 40s (Tuesday night \nand Wednesday night) with surface high pressure settling into Texas. \nA less progressive movement with a farther south track of the upper \nlow would result in lingering rain chances on Tuesday accompanying \nthe cooler air with a longer duration of gusty north to northwest \nwinds. Will continue to monitor model trends with the handling of \nthis system, and anticipate additional changes to the forecast.",
